==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Noun===
[[Image:Circle slices.svg|thumb|right|A geometric sector, to the left.]]
{{en-noun}}

# [[section|Section]].
# [[zone|Zone]] (designated area).
# {{context|geometry|lang=en}} A part of a [[circle]], extending to the [[center]].
# {{context|computing|lang=en}} A fixed-sized unit (traditionally 512 bytes) of sequential data stored on a [[track]] of a digital medium (compare to [[block]]).
# {{context|military|lang=en}} An area [[designated]] by [[boundaries]] within which a [[unit]] operates, and for which it is [[responsible]].
# {{context|military|lang=en}} One of the [[subdivisions]] of a [[coastal]] [[frontier]].
# {{context|science fiction|lang=en}} A [[fictional]] [[region]] of [[space]] designated for [[navigational]] or [[governance]] purposes; for instance, [[W:Sector (Star Trek)]], [[W:List of Star Wars sectors]].
# {{context|calculation|lang=en}} An instrument consisting of two rulers of equal length joined by a hinge.<sup>[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sector_(instrument) W]</sup>
# A field of activity. 
#*{{quote-magazine|date=2013-06-28|author={{w|Joris Luyendijk}}
|volume=189|issue=3|page=21|magazine={{w|The Guardian Weekly}}
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/joris-luyendijk-banking-blog/2013/jun/19/banking-britain-beyond-control Our banks are out of control]
|passage=Seeing the British establishment struggle with the financial '''sector''' is like watching an alcoholic who still resists the idea that something drastic needs to happen for him to turn his life around. Until 2008 there was denial over what finance had become. When a series of bank failures made this impossible, there was widespread anger, leading to the public humiliation of symbolic figures.}}
